WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.320 --> 00:00:01.960
Speaker 1: OK, next.

00:00:01.960 --> 00:00:06.930
I want to talk about, Oh man, I don't even
know how to start this.

00:00:06.930 --> 00:00:11.839
So last week, I interviewed Andrew Yang, a
former Democratic presidential candidate,

00:00:11.839 --> 00:00:15.860
former New York City mayoral candidate.

00:00:15.860 --> 00:00:22.250
During that conversation, one of the topics
I brought up was who from the right?

00:00:22.250 --> 00:00:28.000
Do you imagine would want to be part of your
third party movement and do you welcome everybody

00:00:28.000 --> 00:00:29.000
from the right?

00:00:29.000 --> 00:00:30.679
And he answered the question.

00:00:30.679 --> 00:00:34.550
And then when he said Everybody's welcome,
I followed up and I said, OK, well, what about

00:00:34.550 --> 00:00:36.469
like real extremists like white supremacists?

00:00:36.469 --> 00:00:39.430
And he gave me an answer, and I believe that
he sort of botched the answer.

00:00:39.430 --> 00:00:46.680
And as a result, this has led to absolute
chaos, including Andrew Yang tweeting about,

00:00:46.680 --> 00:00:49.400
Of course, I don't support white supremacy.

00:00:49.400 --> 00:00:51.379
And this is a manufactured controversy.

00:00:51.379 --> 00:00:56.199
Now, it's not clear from his tweet whether
he's attacking me or people reacting to what

00:00:56.199 --> 00:01:01.580
he said to me, but I'm going to play the full
section of the interview for context.

00:01:01.580 --> 00:01:05.019
You will see that I was totally genuine in
my question.

00:01:05.019 --> 00:01:06.110
It was not an attack.

00:01:06.110 --> 00:01:07.909
It was not a manufactured controversy.

00:01:07.909 --> 00:01:08.909
It was not a gotcha.

00:01:08.909 --> 00:01:14.470
It was just me following up to what his answer
was to my first question.

00:01:14.470 --> 00:01:17.860
So let's take a look at the original clip
from last week's interview.

00:01:17.860 --> 00:01:22.170
Who exactly that currently considers themselves
to be on the right?

00:01:22.170 --> 00:01:27.329
Does Andrew Yang imagine bringing into the
forward party and to kind of make it a little

00:01:27.329 --> 00:01:28.329
more specific?

00:01:28.329 --> 00:01:34.650
You know, we would imagine that there's lots
of progressive Democrats, more centrist Democrats,

00:01:34.650 --> 00:01:39.040
even some folks who really consider themselves
centrist, maybe in the kind of line of even

00:01:39.040 --> 00:01:43.590
like a John McCain who like the idea of some
of the reforms you're talking about.

00:01:43.590 --> 00:01:49.079
And that all kind of makes sense to me when
I think about people who believe Donald Trump

00:01:49.079 --> 00:01:50.700
won the election.

00:01:50.700 --> 00:01:57.310
Vaccines, the COVID vaccine magnetize is you
causes autism, whatever the case may be, etc.,

00:01:57.310 --> 00:01:58.310
right?

00:01:58.310 --> 00:02:00.680
You know, the package of ideas that I'm talking
about.

00:02:00.680 --> 00:02:07.180
One, are those people that would even conceivably
say this forward party idea that Andrew Yang

00:02:07.180 --> 00:02:09.470
proposes in a coalition with the left makes
sense.

00:02:09.470 --> 00:02:12.630
And number two, are these even people that
you want as part of your coalition?

00:02:12.630 --> 00:02:17.600
Speaker 2: First, let me say, I want everyone
as part of the coalition, you know, if you

00:02:17.600 --> 00:02:20.160
can vote, we can disagree on a lot of things.

00:02:20.160 --> 00:02:21.160
It's all to the good.

00:02:21.160 --> 00:02:25.700
If we agree on the fact that this duopoly
is not working, it's designed to turn us against

00:02:25.700 --> 00:02:27.280
each other and sink us.

00:02:27.280 --> 00:02:30.890
So that that's one of the principles of the
Foreign Party is that, look, I can disagree

00:02:30.890 --> 00:02:33.900
with you on a lot of stuff, but we can make
common cause on this.

00:02:33.900 --> 00:02:37.640
Then we can go back to disagreeing when the
democracy is actually set up to succeed when

00:02:37.640 --> 00:02:38.740
they're, you know.

00:02:38.740 --> 00:02:40.810
Speaker 1: So let me, I mean, let me ask.

00:02:40.810 --> 00:02:47.090
So even I mean to to make it really specific
if people on the right say the Republican

00:02:47.090 --> 00:02:52.760
Party is too centrist, I'm an extreme white
supremacist or something like that.

00:02:52.760 --> 00:02:57.010
But I think we need to get, you know, we need
single transferable vote or whatever and campaign

00:02:57.010 --> 00:02:58.160
you.

00:02:58.160 --> 00:03:01.140
You still welcome that because you're on the
same page on that one issue.

00:03:01.140 --> 00:03:06.450
Speaker 2: Yeah, if they're going to help
us transition to a more effective democracy.

00:03:06.450 --> 00:03:11.450
You know, I don't need to know what their
stance is on things that I disagree with.

00:03:11.450 --> 00:03:13.290
People have a sense of where I'm coming from.

00:03:13.290 --> 00:03:14.290
OK.

00:03:14.290 --> 00:03:20.780
But right now, I believe that there is an
existential threat to the country as a result

00:03:20.780 --> 00:03:23.850
of the greatest design flaw in the history
of the world.

00:03:23.850 --> 00:03:26.820
OK, so
Speaker 1: his answer, which I don't think

00:03:26.820 --> 00:03:31.450
was a great answer, created a buzz.

00:03:31.450 --> 00:03:35.510
And a lot of people saying, Wow, Andrew Yang
welcomes white supremacy.

00:03:35.510 --> 00:03:37.410
Some people taking it out of context.

00:03:37.410 --> 00:03:39.900
Some people just saying, Wow, that wasn't
a really great answer.

00:03:39.900 --> 00:03:45.280
So Andrew Yang ultimately took to Twitter
a few days after the interview, where he wrote,

00:03:45.280 --> 00:03:48.070
quote, I didn't think this needed stating.

00:03:48.070 --> 00:03:52.960
But people who hold ideologies such as white
supremacy that are antithetical to grace and

00:03:52.960 --> 00:03:56.290
tolerance are not welcome in the forward party.

00:03:56.290 --> 00:04:00.740
Our movement for democracy reform is fighting
to diminish harmful extremism.

00:04:00.740 --> 00:04:04.620
Now, remember in the interview with me, he
said that it would be fine, that they would

00:04:04.620 --> 00:04:07.100
be welcome as long as they're OK with ranked
choice voting.

00:04:07.100 --> 00:04:08.100
That's what he said.

00:04:08.100 --> 00:04:10.010
I didn't trick them into saying that.

00:04:10.010 --> 00:04:15.790
He followed up with another tweet which said,
quote Relatedly, this kind of manufactured

00:04:15.790 --> 00:04:20.170
controversy is the kind of thing that's dragging
down our public discourse and distracting

00:04:20.170 --> 00:04:21.821
us from the true issues at hand.

00:04:21.821 --> 00:04:26.080
He gave a bad answer Racist is not the path
to enlightenment now.

00:04:26.080 --> 00:04:32.760
I didn't know whether he was accusing me of
manufactured controversy or others on Twitter

00:04:32.760 --> 00:04:34.400
of manufactured controversy.

00:04:34.400 --> 00:04:39.440
But in response to this, I tweeted, Assuming
this tweet is a response to the question I

00:04:39.440 --> 00:04:43.900
asked during our interview, I hope it needs
no clarification that I was not attempting

00:04:43.900 --> 00:04:48.540
to manufacture controversy and ask the question
completely, genuinely.

00:04:48.540 --> 00:04:54.220
After reading the book now after the interview,
Andrew and I spoke off air for a bit and he

00:04:54.220 --> 00:04:57.070
said, Your question about white supremacy.

00:04:57.070 --> 00:04:59.310
It actually got me thinking.

00:04:59.310 --> 00:05:01.540
And he said he hadn't considered it.

00:05:01.540 --> 00:05:02.560
He came up with the answer.

00:05:02.560 --> 00:05:05.310
You see him pause when he answers the question.

00:05:05.310 --> 00:05:08.210
He didn't say it was unfair or a gotcha or
manufactured controversy.

00:05:08.210 --> 00:05:13.430
We had a perfectly nice conversation, so I
hope and am leaning towards.

00:05:13.430 --> 00:05:17.830
He was talking about people on Twitter weaponizing
his answer.

00:05:17.830 --> 00:05:23.430
Now that being said, I think he just did not
answer the question that, well, if I were

00:05:23.430 --> 00:05:27.730
him, the way I would have answered that question
would have been to say, David, listen, I've

00:05:27.730 --> 00:05:35.140
denounced white supremacy many times during
the last few years.

00:05:35.140 --> 00:05:40.130
It's hard to imagine too many white supremacists
wanting to join a movement led by an Asian

00:05:40.130 --> 00:05:46.700
guy, but I can't control to whom these principles
appeal.

00:05:46.700 --> 00:05:51.820
And just like any candidate, might end up
with anybody supporting them.

00:05:51.820 --> 00:05:56.100
I can't control that, but a my inviting white
supremacists.

00:05:56.100 --> 00:05:57.450
Of course not.

00:05:57.450 --> 00:06:00.020
Do I think white supremacists will even be
interested in this movement?

00:06:00.020 --> 00:06:01.020
Of course not.

00:06:01.020 --> 00:06:02.510
That's how I would have answered the question.

00:06:02.510 --> 00:06:06.360
He answered it a different way, which was
to say, Yeah, if they want ranked choice voting,

00:06:06.360 --> 00:06:08.430
then they're welcome.

00:06:08.430 --> 00:06:12.580
So what is this ultimately really about?

00:06:12.580 --> 00:06:14.980
For me, it's about.

00:06:14.980 --> 00:06:20.200
Who exactly from the right are we looking
to pull in to this forward party?

00:06:20.200 --> 00:06:25.050
And we had a good conversation about that,
and I don't know that necessarily more has

00:06:25.050 --> 00:06:28.950
to be said, but I'd be curious to your reaction
to the entire exchange.

